From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nikolajs Agafonovs <nikolajs.agafonovs@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 16:44:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] x86_64: __int128 and unsigned __int128 (VT_QLONG as a type,
 two long long words)

The 128-bit integer types of gcc on x86-64, so that code written for
them (the Linux kernel's u128/s128: cmpxchg128, freelist_aba_t,
mul_u64_u64_shr, gf128hash) compiles unmodified.  __SIZEOF_INT128__ is
predefined as 16 (the kernel's CC_HAS_INT128 probe redefines it under
-Werror); __int128_t and __uint128_t of tccdefs.h become the real types.

VT_QLONG, until now only the ABI class of a 16-byte integer structure
(two INTEGER eightbytes), is the type: size 16, alignment 16, an integer
in is_integer_btype(), the usual arithmetic conversions rank it above
long long, type_to_str() prints it.  A value is held in two registers
(r, r2) exactly like a long long on the 32-bit targets, so the two-word
machinery of tccgen.c -- lexpand(), lbuild(), gv_dup() and gen_opl(),
now gen_opw() -- is parametrised on the word type (VT_WORD1: int or
long long) and width instead of being compiled under PTR_SIZE == 4 only:
on x86-64 it works on two long long words.  + - * & | ^ and the
comparisons come out of it unchanged (the 64x64->128 low product is the
backend's TOK_UMULL, mul), shifts by a constant too; a shift by a
variable is inline (gen_shift128(): shld/shrd and a test of bit 6 of the
count, as gen_shift64() on i386); / and % call __divti3, __modti3,
__udivti3, __umodti3, written for libtcc1 in 64-bit arithmetic on a
two-word structure (the same ABI as unsigned __int128).  A constant
whose high word is loaded with xor would lose the carry between the
halves of + and -: gen_opw() loads it first.

Casts: widening puts zero or the sign of the 64-bit value into the high
register (narrower sources are widened to 64 bits first, pointers are
zero-extended); narrowing keeps the low word; a 128-bit lvalue cast to a
narrower type is read as its low bytes.  Constants stay 64-bit: an
__int128 constant is its sign-extended low word, fold_int128() folds
only what that holds exactly (overflow, a logical shift of a negative
word, an unsigned division of a value above 2^63 go to run time), an
unsigned 64-bit constant at or above 2^63 is materialised in registers,
in a static initializer it is refused.  The backend handles a VT_QLONG
word as 64 bits (is64_type), classifies the type as INTEGER (two
registers, 16-byte alignment on the stack; gfunc_call pushes both words
when the registers are out).

Refused with a message: conversions to and from floating point, an asm
operand of 128 bits in a register (a memory constraint is fine, the
kernel passes the 64-bit halves), bitfields (the existing check), atomic
builtins of 16 bytes (the existing size check); kprop does not track
__int128 locals.  Only x86-64 SysV (TCC_HAS_INT128): i386 and the other
targets are untouched and say "'__int128' is not supported on this
target".
---
